    "chunk_text": "The CBSL conducts monetary policy in a forward looking and data-driven manner. Accordingly, decision on whether to increase, decrease or maintain policy interest rates is made at each monetary policy review, taking into account the latest available economic data, forecasts and risk assessments. The Governor\u2019s remarks were intended to convey this established policy framework. They did not constitute any commitment or indication regarding the future path or level of policy interest rates.\u201d\n\n2026 2027 2028 2029\n\nSource of Financing: Borrowing- Sovereign Bonds (USD mn)\n\n- 1,500 1,500 -\n\nInternational Domestic Total\n\nEnd May, 2026 1.8 2.3 4.1\n\n1241 1242\n\n\u0db4\u0daf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dc2\u0dd2\u0dd9 \u0dca \u0dad\u0dd4\u0dcf\n\nBasically, these data are sensitive and are not an indication of the future.\n\n7. \u2022 Policy on reserve accumulation and exchange rate flexibility The Central Bank of Sri Lanka maintains a flexible exchange rate regime in line with Flexible Inflation Targeting - FIT- framework. Under the flexible exchange rate regime, the exchange rate is determined based on the demand and supply conditions in the domestic foreign exchange market, and the Central Bank\u2019s intervention in the market is limited only to mitigate excessive volatility in the exchange rate and to build up reserves through purchases of foreign exchange from the market. As mandated by the Public Debt Management Act, No. 33 of 2024, Liability Management Operations - LMOs - are to be undertaken with the objective of mitigating refinancing risk, interest rate risk and currency risk associated with the Government debt portfolio. Accordingly, the Public Debt Management Office - PDMO - is currently taking necessary steps to strengthen the regulatory framework governing Liability Management Operations and is also assessing opportune market conditions and available instruments to undertake such operations. So, what operations and strategies are to be taken will be announced then and there, because these are highly sensitive information."
