    "chunk_size_bytes": 1665,
    "chunk_text": "7. \u2022 Policy on reserve accumulation and exchange rate flexibility The Central Bank of Sri Lanka maintains a flexible exchange rate regime in line with Flexible Inflation Targeting - FIT- framework. Under the flexible exchange rate regime, the exchange rate is determined based on the demand and supply conditions in the domestic foreign exchange market, and the Central Bank\u2019s intervention in the market is limited only to mitigate excessive volatility in the exchange rate and to build up reserves through purchases of foreign exchange from the market. As mandated by the Public Debt Management Act, No. 33 of 2024, Liability Management Operations - LMOs - are to be undertaken with the objective of mitigating refinancing risk, interest rate risk and currency risk associated with the Government debt portfolio. Accordingly, the Public Debt Management Office - PDMO - is currently taking necessary steps to strengthen the regulatory framework governing Liability Management Operations and is also assessing opportune market conditions and available instruments to undertake such operations. So, what operations and strategies are to be taken will be announced then and there, because these are highly sensitive information.\n\nAnd, details of the previous distributions are given in the Table below.\n\nCentral Bank Distributable Profit and Profit Transfers to Government: 2015-2025\n\nRs. Mn\n\nSpecially, in 2016, 2017, 2019, 2020 and 2021, some dividends had been remitted to the Government.\n\nThank you very much.\n\nYear Profit Transfers to\n\n2015 - 2016 6,378.27 2017 15,000.00 2018 - 2019 24,008.98 2020 15,016.74 2021 31,035.79 2022 - 2023 - 2024 - 2025 44,985.19"
