    "md5": "4e5bdf5e04ee36f9048224ba49f49684",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1682,
    "chunk_text": "Specially, in 2016, 2017, 2019, 2020 and 2021, some dividends had been remitted to the Government.\n\nThank you very much.\n\nYear Profit Transfers to\n\n2015 - 2016 6,378.27 2017 15,000.00 2018 - 2019 24,008.98 2020 15,016.74 2021 31,035.79 2022 - 2023 - 2024 - 2025 44,985.19\n\nThe Central Bank has made a profit of about Rs. 200 billion and how much of that profit have they given to the Government is the question I would like to ask first. Talking about reserves, you accept that there is only US Dollars 6,800 million of reserves. After two years - it was US Dollars 6,100 million then - and getting three tranches from the IMF, which amounts to about US Dollars 1.1 billion, when you add those two amounts alone, it should be at least US Dollars 7.2 billion in hand. So, how is there a downward trend in reserves, when there should be an upward trend? Where is the FDI? When is the interest rates coming down? When is the rupee strengthening? Hon. Minister, we also would like to see a strong economy. One US Dollar in hand is far better than US Dollars one billion in the air.\n\nNow, you could see the HSBC leaving. That was something I did when I was the Minister of Trade, Commerce and Consumer Affairs. We went to UK and got these 4,000 seats. Donnelley, which is a US company, is going out of the country. Hon. Minister, I would like you to emphasize as to how we could protect investments in this country. Do not let any investments go out. I do not want to dampen the spirits, but prefer any investment in the country to be retained rather than no investments coming in. But, I do not think a conducive climate is being created by you all in order to ensure that FDIs come in."
